### Step 1: Locate the image of point A

Using the lens formula \(\frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f}\).
\frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f}
Given object distance \(u = -30\text{ cm}\) (real object, left of lens) and focal length \(f = 20\text{ cm}\) (convex lens).
\frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{-30} = \frac{1}{20}
\frac{1}{v} + \frac{1}{30} = \frac{1}{20}
\frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{20} - \frac{1}{30} = \frac{3 - 2}{60} = \frac{1}{60}
v = 60\text{ cm}
The image A' of point A is formed at \(60\text{ cm}\) on the opposite side of the lens.

### Step 2: Calculate the transverse magnification (m)

Transverse magnification \(m = \frac{v}{u}\).
m = \frac{60}{-30} = -2
The negative sign indicates an inverted image.

### Step 3: Calculate the longitudinal magnification (m_L) and image dimensions

Longitudinal magnification \(m_L = \frac{dv}{du} = m^2\) for small objects.
The horizontal extent of the object is \(du = 1\text{ cm}\).
dv = m^2 du = (-2)^2 \times 1 = 4 \times 1 = 4\text{ cm}
The vertical height of the object is \(h_0 = 2\text{ cm}\).
h_i = m h_0 = -2 \times 2 = -4\text{ cm}
So, the image A'B' has a horizontal extent of \(4\text{ cm}\) and a vertical height of \(4\text{ cm}\) (inverted).

### Step 4: Determine the angle made by the image with the principal axis

The angle \(\theta\) made by the image with the principal axis can be found using the tangent of the angle.
\tan \theta_{\text{image}} = \frac{\text{vertical height of image}}{\text{horizontal extent of image}}
Considering the inverted nature of the image, the vertical height is \(-4\text{ cm}\) and the horizontal extent is \(4\text{ cm}\).
\tan \theta_{\text{image}} = \frac{-4\text{ cm}}{4\text{ cm}} = -1
\theta_{\text{image}} = \arctan(-1) = -45^{\circ}
The angle made by the image with the principal axis is \(-45^{\circ}\).

The problem describes a drone camera capturing a landscape. We are given the height of the drone (object distance), the size of the camera film (image size), and the area of the landscape photographed (object area). We need to find the focal length of the lens.
• Height of drone (object distance), \(H = 18 \text{ km}\)
• Size of camera film (image size) \(= 2 \text{ cm} \times 2 \text{ cm}\)
• Area of landscape photographed (object area) \(= 400 \text{ km}^2\)
First, we convert the given areas into linear dimensions (side lengths) and ensure consistent units (meters).
x = \sqrt{400 \text{ km}^2} = 20 \text{ km} = 20 \times 10^3 \text{ m} = 2 \times 10^4 \text{ m}
y = \sqrt{2 \text{ cm} \times 2 \text{ cm}} = 2 \text{ cm} = 2 \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}
For a lens, the ratio of object size to image size is equal to the ratio of object distance to image distance (focal length in this case, assuming the image is formed at the focal plane for a distant object). This is derived from similar triangles as shown in the diagram.
\frac{x}{y} = \frac{H}{f}
Substitute the calculated values into the magnification formula:
\frac{2 \times 10^4 \text{ m}}{2 \times 10^{-2} \text{ m}} = \frac{18 \text{ km}}{f}
Convert \(18 \text{ km}\) to meters: \(18 \text{ km} = 18 \times 10^3 \text{ m}\).
10^6 = \frac{18 \times 10^3 \text{ m}}{f}
f = \frac{18 \times 10^3}{10^6} \text{ m} = 18 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}
Convert \(f\) from meters to millimeters and then to centimeters:
f = 18 \text{ mm}
f = 1.8 \text{ cm}
The focal length of the lens in the drone camera is 1.8 cm.

### Detailed Solution


### Step 1: Identify given values and convert to SI units

Given focal lengths are \(f_{1} = 30 \text{ cm}\) and \(f_{2} = 10 \text{ cm}\). The distance between the lenses is \(d = 10 \text{ cm}\).
Converting these to meters for power calculation (Diopters):
f_{1} = 0.3 \text{ m}
f_{2} = 0.1 \text{ m}
d = 0.1 \text{ m}

### Step 2: Apply the formula for the equivalent focal length of two thin lenses separated by a distance

The formula for the equivalent focal length (\(f_{\text{eq}}\)) of two thin lenses separated by a distance \(d\) is:
\frac{1}{f_{\text{eq}}} = \frac{1}{f_{1}} + \frac{1}{f_{2}} - \frac{d}{f_{1}f_{2}}

### Step 3: Substitute the values into the formula

\frac{1}{f_{\text{eq}}} = \frac{1}{0.3} + \frac{1}{0.1} - \frac{0.1}{(0.3)(0.1)}

### Step 4: Perform the calculation

\frac{1}{f_{\text{eq}}} = \frac{10}{3} + 10 - \frac{0.1}{0.03}
\frac{1}{f_{\text{eq}}} = \frac{10}{3} + 10 - \frac{10}{3}
\frac{1}{f_{\text{eq}}} = 10

### Step 5: Calculate the power of the combination

The power (\(P\)) of the combination is the reciprocal of the equivalent focal length in meters:
\text{Power} = \frac{1}{f_{\text{eq}}} = 10 \text{ D}

### Given Data

Refractive index of liquid A, \(n_1 = 1.3\)
Refractive index of liquid B, \(n_2 = 1.4\)
Object distance, \(u = -13\text{ cm}\) (object is in medium A, and placed to the left of the vertex)
Magnification, \(m = -2\)

### Diagram of the setup


### Applying the refraction formula at a spherical surface

\frac{n_2}{v} - \frac{n_1}{u} = \frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}
Substitute the given values:
\frac{1.4}{v} - \frac{1.3}{-13} = \frac{1.4 - 1.3}{R}
\frac{1.4}{v} + \frac{1}{10} = \frac{0.1}{R}
\frac{1.4}{v} = \frac{0.1}{R} - \frac{1}{10} = \frac{1}{10R} - \frac{1}{10} = \frac{1-R}{10R} \quad \ldots(1)

### Using the magnification formula

The magnification for refraction at a spherical surface is given by:
m = \frac{v/n_2}{u/n_1}
Substitute the given magnification and refractive indices:
-2 = \frac{v/1.4}{-13/1.3}
-2 = \frac{v/1.4}{-10}
20 = \frac{v}{1.4}
v = 20 \times 1.4 = 28\text{ cm}

### Calculating the radius of curvature (R)

Substitute the value of \(v\) into equation (1):
\frac{1.4}{28} = \frac{1-R}{10R}
\frac{1}{20} = \frac{1-R}{10R}
10R = 20(1-R)
10R = 20 - 20R
30R = 20
R = \frac{20}{30} = \frac{2}{3}\text{ cm}
The radius of curvature of the meniscus is \(\frac{2}{3}\text{ cm}\).

### Step 1: Analyze the lens system and identify parameters

The system consists of a liquid lens and a glass lens in contact. We need to find the equivalent focal length of this combination. The refractive index of the liquid is \(\mu_l = 1.3\) and for the glass lens is \(\mu_g = 1.5\). The radii of curvature are \(30\) cm and \(20\) cm.

### Step 2: Calculate the focal length of the liquid lens

The concave surface of the glass lens is upwards and filled with liquid. This forms a plano-concave liquid lens. The upper surface of the liquid is flat (air-liquid interface), so its radius of curvature is \(R_1 = \infty\). The lower surface of the liquid is concave, matching the upper surface of the glass lens, so its radius of curvature is \(R_2 = -30\) cm (using sign convention where light travels from left to right, and center of curvature is to the left).
\frac{1}{f_l} = (\mu_l - 1) \left(\frac{1}{R_1} - \frac{1}{R_2}\right)
\frac{1}{f_l} = (1.3 - 1) \left(\frac{1}{\infty} - \frac{1}{-30}\right)
\frac{1}{f_l} = 0.3 \left(0 + \frac{1}{30}\right) = \frac{0.3}{30} = \frac{1}{100} \text{ cm}^{-1}

### Step 3: Calculate the focal length of the glass lens

For the glass lens, based on the solution's interpretation, the radii of curvature are \(R_1 = -30\) cm (concave surface in contact with liquid) and \(R_2 = -20\) cm (concave surface facing downwards). This implies a concave meniscus lens.
\frac{1}{f_g} = (\mu_g - 1) \left(\frac{1}{R_1} - \frac{1}{R_2}\right)
\frac{1}{f_g} = (1.5 - 1) \left(\frac{1}{-30} - \frac{1}{-20}\right)
\frac{1}{f_g} = 0.5 \left(-\frac{1}{30} + \frac{1}{20}\right) = 0.5 \left(\frac{-2+3}{60}\right) = 0.5 \left(\frac{1}{60}\right) = \frac{0.5}{60} = \frac{1}{120} \text{ cm}^{-1}

### Step 4: Calculate the focal length of the combination

For lenses in contact, the equivalent focal length \(F\) is given by the sum of their powers:
\frac{1}{F} = \frac{1}{f_l} + \frac{1}{f_g}
\frac{1}{F} = \frac{1}{100} + \frac{1}{120}
\frac{1}{F} = \frac{6}{600} + \frac{5}{600} = \frac{6+5}{600} = \frac{11}{600} \text{ cm}^{-1}
F = \frac{600}{11} \text{ cm}
The focal length of the liquid-glass combination is \(\frac{600}{11}\) cm.

### Step 1: Identify given parameters and formula for refraction at spherical surface

Given refractive indices are \(n_1 = 1\) (for the medium where the object is) and \(n_2 = 1.4\) (for the medium where the image is formed). The radius of curvature of the spherical interface is \(R\). The object (point source) is placed at a distance of \(4R\) in front of the spherical interface. According to the Cartesian sign convention, the object distance \(u = -4R\). Since the interface is convex towards the object (as implied by the diagram where the center of curvature is to the right), the radius of curvature \(R = +R\).
The formula for refraction at a spherical surface is:
\frac{n_2}{v} - \frac{n_1}{u} = \frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}

### Step 2: Calculate the image distance \(v\)

Substitute the given values into the refraction formula:
\frac{1.4}{v} - \frac{1}{-4R} = \frac{1.4 - 1}{R}
\frac{1.4}{v} + \frac{1}{4R} = \frac{0.4}{R}
Rearrange the equation to solve for \(v\):
\frac{1.4}{v} = \frac{0.4}{R} - \frac{1}{4R}
To subtract the terms on the right side, find a common denominator:
\frac{1.4}{v} = \frac{0.4 \times 4}{4R} - \frac{1}{4R} = \frac{1.6}{4R} - \frac{1}{4R} = \frac{0.6}{4R}
\frac{1.4}{v} = \frac{0.6}{4R}
Now, solve for \(v\):
v = \frac{1.4 \times 4R}{0.6} = \frac{5.6R}{0.6} = \frac{56R}{6} = \frac{28R}{3}

### Step 3: Calculate the magnification \(m\)

The formula for magnification for a spherical refracting surface is:
m = \frac{n_1}{n_2} \frac{v}{u}
Substitute the values of \(n_1, n_2, v\) and \(u\):
m = \frac{1}{1.4} \times \frac{\frac{28R}{3}}{-4R}
Simplify the expression:
m = \frac{1}{1.4} \times \frac{28R}{3 \times (-4R)} = \frac{1}{1.4} \times \frac{28}{-12}
m = \frac{1}{1.4} \times \left(-\frac{7}{3}\right)
m = -\frac{7}{1.4 \times 3} = -\frac{7}{4.2} \approx -1.666...
The question asks for the magnitude of the magnification.
|m| = |-1.666...| \approx 1.67
The magnitude of the magnification is approximately 1.67.

### Case 1: Lenses combined without any gap

Given focal length of convex lens, \(f_1 = +5\) cm
Given focal length of concave lens, \(f_2 = -4\) cm
Distance between lenses, \(d = 0\) cm
Object distance, \(u = -10\) cm
Equivalent focal length \(F_1\) of the combination is given by:
\frac{1}{F_1} = \frac{1}{f_1} + \frac{1}{f_2} - \frac{d}{f_1 f_2}
\frac{1}{F_1} = \frac{1}{5} + \frac{1}{-4} - \frac{0}{(5)(-4)}
\frac{1}{F_1} = \frac{1}{5} - \frac{1}{4} = \frac{4 - 5}{20} = -\frac{1}{20}
F_1 = -20\text{ cm}
Using the lens formula for the combination to find the image distance \(v_1\):
\frac{1}{v_1} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{F_1}
\frac{1}{v_1} = \frac{1}{F_1} + \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{-20} + \frac{1}{-10}
\frac{1}{v_1} = -\frac{1}{20} - \frac{2}{20} = -\frac{3}{20}
v_1 = -\frac{20}{3}\text{ cm}
Magnification \(m_1\) for the combined system:
m_1 = \frac{v_1}{u} = \frac{-20/3}{-10} = \frac{20}{30} = \frac{2}{3}

### Case 2: Lenses separated by a gap of 1 cm

Distance between lenses, \(d = 1\) cm
For the first lens (convex, \(f_1 = +5\) cm):
u_1 = -10\text{ cm}
Using the lens formula:
\frac{1}{v_1'} - \frac{1}{u_1} = \frac{1}{f_1}
\frac{1}{v_1'} = \frac{1}{f_1} + \frac{1}{u_1} = \frac{1}{5} + \frac{1}{-10}
\frac{1}{v_1'} = \frac{2}{10} - \frac{1}{10} = \frac{1}{10}
v_1' = +10\text{ cm}
Magnification by the first lens:
m_1' = \frac{v_1'}{u_1} = \frac{10}{-10} = -1
For the second lens (concave, \(f_2 = -4\) cm):
The image formed by the first lens acts as the object for the second lens. This image is formed 10 cm to the right of the convex lens. Since the concave lens is 1 cm away from the convex lens, the object distance for the concave lens \(u_2\) is:
u_2 = v_1' - d = 10 - 1 = +9\text{ cm}
(The object for the second lens is virtual as it is to the right of the concave lens)
Using the lens formula for the second lens:
\frac{1}{v_2'} - \frac{1}{u_2} = \frac{1}{f_2}
\frac{1}{v_2'} = \frac{1}{f_2} + \frac{1}{u_2} = \frac{1}{-4} + \frac{1}{9}
\frac{1}{v_2'} = \frac{-9 + 4}{36} = -\frac{5}{36}
v_2' = -\frac{36}{5}\text{ cm}
Magnification by the second lens:
m_2' = \frac{v_2'}{u_2} = \frac{-36/5}{9} = -\frac{36}{5 \times 9} = -\frac{4}{5}
Total magnification \(m_2\) for the separated system:
m_2 = m_1' \times m_2' = (-1) \times \left(-\frac{4}{5}\right) = \frac{4}{5}

### Final Calculation

The value of \(\left|\frac{m_1}{m_2}\right|\) is:
\left|\frac{m_1}{m_2}\right| = \left|\frac{2/3}{4/5}\right| = \left|\frac{2}{3} \times \frac{5}{4}\right| = \left|\frac{10}{12}\right| = \left|\frac{5}{6}\right| = \frac{5}{6}

